## Coordinate convention

The `bbox` from the `documents/locate` response uses **PDF points with a top-left origin** — the same convention as the HTML canvas [2]. Each bbox is `[x0, top, x1, bottom]` in PDF points (1/72 inch), with the origin at the **top-left** of the page [2].

## Why you must avoid `convertToViewportPoint()`

PDF.js's `viewport.convertToViewportPoint()` expects **native PDF coordinates**, which use a **bottom-left origin** [1]. Since `bbox` already uses a top-left origin, feeding it into that helper would apply an incorrect axis conversion.

Instead, a **single multiplication by the scale factor** is enough — with **no** axis conversion [1]:

```js
const [x0, top, x1, bottom] = segment.bbox;
highlight.style.left   = `${x0 * scale}px`;
highlight.style.top    = `${top * scale}px`;
highlight.style.width  = `${(x1 - x0) * scale}px`;
highlight.style.height = `${(bottom - top) * scale}px`;
```

where `scale = canvas width / page width in points` (the same scale used to render the page) [1]. This simple multiplication is correct for **non-rotated pages** [1].

> Note: this guidance is descriptive. If you have a conflicting ADR or standard, let me know and I can flag it.

